\section{Interpretability Case Study}

Stocks selected at the end of January 2025 are chosen as a case study, combining SHAP [Lundberg and Lee, 2017] attribution and large language models to generate interpretable results. SHAP attribution identifies core features: Q4 2024 net profit growth (+0.35), PE valuation (+0.21), and one-month volatility (-0.18).

\textbf{LLM-generated explanation:} The core logic for this stock's selection is:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Q4 2024 net profit year-on-year growth reached 21\%, significantly above industry average, with sufficient earnings growth momentum, contributing positively to ranking score;
    \item Current PE valuation is 6.8x, at the 12th percentile over the past 5 years, indicating high valuation safety margin and improved stock selection priority;
    \item One-month return volatility is lower than 80\% of stocks, with lower downside risk, meeting low-risk preference stock selection requirements.
\end{enumerate}

Overall, this stock demonstrates good earnings growth, low valuation, and low volatility, meeting the strategy's stock selection criteria. This case illustrates that the constructed framework can output understandable stock selection logic, satisfying institutional investors' requirements for decision traceability.

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

To address the problems of misaligned optimization objectives, poor single-model stability, and insufficient interpretability in quantitative stock selection, this paper proposes a quantitative stock selection framework based on return-aware ranking learning and multi-model fusion. Custom loss functions align optimization objectives with investment goals, RRF fusion improves stock selection stability, and LLM integration enables interpretable recommendations. Empirical results demonstrate that the proposed method significantly outperforms traditional methods in the A-share market, providing a new feasible solution for quantitative investment.

Future research can be extended in two directions:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Introduce high-frequency factors and alternative data (e.g., sentiment analysis from social media) to enhance the model's ability to capture short-term returns;
    \item Explore dynamic fusion weights that automatically adjust the fusion proportion of different models according to different market environments, further improving strategy adaptability.
\end{enumerate}

